{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"melting-resting-serpent","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nDiscovered while fixing 1a47-61a4-a981-4398 (the review-bot stop_grace_period). That ticket\nraised the grace period so an in-flight store write has room to finish, but two unbounded\nresidues remain that make any grace-period constant unprovable. Both are in the review path,\nnot in compose, so they were left out of scope there.\n\n**1. The store write runs ON the asyncio event loop, so the declared bounds cannot fire.**\n`voter.py` calls the synchronous `emit_code_review_artifact` (a plain `def`) directly from the\n`review_and_vote` coroutine — no `await`, no `asyncio.to_thread`. Every store write it performs\n(`create_ticket`, `sidecar.emit`, one `link` per trailer ref) therefore blocks the loop thread.\n\nProven locally with two experiments:\n  * Entering/exiting `app.lifespan` with `SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS=0.2` and a worker doing a\n    3.0s SYNCHRONOUS block took **3.00s** to shut down — the declared bound did not bound it.\n  * A heartbeat task ticking every 0.1s showed a single **2.01s gap** across a 2.0s synchronous\n    review — the loop is blocked for the whole write.\n\nBecause the loop is blocked, `asyncio.wait_for(queue.join(), timeout=_shutdown_drain_seconds())`\ncannot fire, `task.cancel()` cannot be delivered, and the following `await task` is bounded by\nnothing. The same applies to `_worker`s `asyncio.wait_for(review_and_vote(...), timeout=\nreview_timeout_seconds())` for the on-loop region, and to `/health`, which cannot answer while a\nwrite is running.\n\nThis falsifies three stated invariants: the `app.py` module docstring on the bounded drain, the\n`docker-compose.yml` comment that the grace period need only exceed `SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS`, and\nthe docstring of `tests/unit/test_review_bot.py::test_lifespan_drain_is_bounded` (\"a review that\nnever returns must not hang shutdown\"). That existing test passes only because it blocks the\nworker at `await asyncio.sleep(3600)` — an await point, where cancellation lands instantly.\n\n**2. The lock-held git calls have no subprocess timeout.**\n`_store/event_append.py` runs `git add` / `git commit` via bare `subprocess.run(...)` with no\n`timeout=`, INSIDE `with lock.write_lock(...)`. `_store/push.py` passes `timeout=_GIT_TIMEOUT`\nfor the same class of call, so this is an inconsistency rather than a deliberate choice. A slow\nor contended tracker volume can hold the mkdir write lock indefinitely.\n\nTogether these mean the worst-case in-flight store write is not computable, so no\n`stop_grace_period` can be shown sufficient — only made generously large, which is what 1a47 did.\n\n## Approach\n\nBound the shutdown path so a worst case exists, then re-derive the grace period from it. Likely\nshape: move the blocking emission off the event loop so the existing `wait_for` bounds become\nenforceable, bound the lifespan cancel/await, and give the lock-held git calls the same\n`_GIT_TIMEOUT` treatment `push.py` already uses. Note that moving work to a thread does not by\nitself make it cancellable, so the lifespan bound is required too.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/review_bot/voter.py` — the on-loop `emit_code_review_artifact` call.\n- `src/rebar/review_bot/app.py` — the lifespan cancel/await bound.\n- `src/rebar/_store/event_append.py` — subprocess timeouts on the lock-held git calls.\n- `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml` — re-derive `stop_grace_period` once a worst case exists.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A worker blocked in SYNCHRONOUS code no longer prevents the drain bound from firing;\n      regression-tested with a synchronous (not await-based) worker, which the existing\n      `test_lifespan_drain_is_bounded` does not cover.\n- [ ] The lifespan cancel + await path is bounded end to end, so total shutdown has an upper\n      bound that can be stated.\n- [ ] The lock-held git calls in `event_append.py` carry a timeout, matching `push.py`.\n- [ ] `stop_grace_period` is re-derived from the now-computable worst case, and the honest-limit\n      note in `docker-compose.yml` is removed or updated.","id":"c2ba-98bf-ca2d-481a","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"review-bot shutdown and per-review timeouts are unenforceable: the store write runs synchronously on the event loop, and its lock-held git calls have no timeout"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785533447060991001,"uuid":"d4eb571b-aeb1-4be4-a6bf-18669b74df12"}